/*
# DataGrid

Records as rows, one column per field you choose: headers that sort, reorder and resize by
drag (touch-correct), selection and keyboard travel from `Table`, and a windowed collection,
so ten thousand rows cost a screenful of views.

A DataGrid has **no children**. What it shows comes from two attributes: `items`, the
records, one row each; and `columns`, plain records, one column each.

```declare
App [ width = 440, height = 220,
    d: Dataset { { "rows": [ { "id": 1, "title": "Fix the rail",      "state": "open" },
                             { "id": 2, "title": "Draft the chapter", "state": "done" },
                             { "id": 3, "title": "Measure the grid",  "state": "open" } ] } },
    DataGrid [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 400, height = 160, selects = "multi",
        items = { app.d.value.rows },
        columns = { [ { id: "id",    title: "ID",    width: 50 },
                      { id: "title", title: "Title", width: 220 },
                      { id: "state", title: "State", width: 110 } ] } ]
    ]
```

Drag a header sideways to reorder; drag the divider between two headers to resize; click a
header to sort.

- **Items** are records, kept matched to their rows by each record's `id`. Taken from a
  `Dataset`, they are the dataset's own records: an edit made in a cell writes the record,
  and everything that reads it follows.
- **Columns** are records with an `id` (the field the column shows, and its name), a
  `title`, a `width`, and optionally a `cell`, an `editor` or `edit`. Being data, they can be
  built, stored, or chosen by the person, like any records.
- **A cell** shows its field as plain text — `String(value)`, from the left, in the theme's
  `gridFontSize` — with no formatting and no alignment of its own. Money, dates, and numbers
  set to the right are a class that formats and places the text:
  `{ id: "amount", cell: "MoneyCell" }` with
  `class MoneyCell [ width = 100%, height = 100%, TextLabel [ x = end, padding = [0, 8, 0, 8], fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize }, text = { money(:amount) } ] ]`.
- **A cell of your own:** a column that names a class (`cell: "DoneCell"`)
  draws that class in every row of the column, each copy bound to its own row's record (so
  `:done` is that row's `done`). One class can serve several columns: it reads which it is
  in as `provided("column")` — `{ id, title }` — and the field as `:@[(provided("column").id)]`.
  Enter or F2 on such a cell enters it: the focus goes to the first focusable view a person
  can see inside it (shown, and not at `opacity` 0), in the order they are written (Space presses that control instead), and a cell
  with none is not entered. A field of your own in a cell answers its keys first: Escape reaches what the cell holds,
  then the grid's own bindings, and only then leaves the cell; a single-line field's Return
  runs its `onEnter` before anything else hears it. Cells in one column can differ by row: the class decides
  how to look for its record, with a `State` or a `classFor` inside it.
- **Editing selects first.** A press selects a cell; Enter, F2 or a second press on the
  selected cell enters it, and Escape or Enter leaves it (Enter a row down; Tab and Shift-Tab
  leave it a cell along, wrapping rows). A column's
  `editor` names the class a cell becomes while it is edited, beside the `cell` it shows the
  rest of the time — `{ id: "state", cell: "StateText", editor: "StatePicker" }` — bound to
  the row's record like the cell, with its first control given the keyboard. A cell entered
  by Enter or F2 starts with its text selected, in the grid's own field or a text field (a
  combobox's included) in your editor, so what is typed replaces it; a second press puts the
  caret where it landed. What your editor does not answer of Escape, Enter and Tab leaves it.
- **`edit: true`** is the grid's own editor, a text field writing the record as you type:
  typing on a selected cell replaces its text and opens the edit — an accent, an Option
  letter or an input method's word composes there as in any field — Up and Down end it and
  move a row, Esc puts the text back. With `commitOn: "end"` beside it, a cell writes once,
  when the edit ends (Enter, or leaving the cell), and Esc drops it. Either way the write
  goes through `applyEdit`, which a program overrides to send it to a service.
- **A heading row** is a record with a `heading` field — `{ id: "g1", heading: "Overdue" }` —
  drawn across the full width, with no cells, passed over by selection and the arrow keys.
  `headingClass` names a class of your own to draw it.
- **Sorting names a derivation.** A header click sets `sortOn` and `sortDir`; your app
  derives the sorted items from them, and the grid shows the order it is given.
- **Order and widths** are the grid's state, `order` and `widths`, delivered through
  `applyColumnOrder` and `applyColumnWidth`, so an app can persist a person's layout. A
  width of `0` hides a column.
- **Rows** are the theme's `gridRowHeight` tall (28), the header `gridHeaderHeight` (30), and
  the text `gridFontSize`: set them in a theme to change every grid.
- **Selection** is `Table`'s (a press, Shift for a range, ⌘ or Ctrl to add one, the arrows,
  ⌘-walk with Space, ⌘A or Ctrl+A for every row). A Select All button calls `selectAll()`.

## items
The records, one row each, in the order shown. Usually a dataset's list —
`items = { app.tasks.value.rows }` — or one derived from it (sorted, filtered, grouped). A
record whose `heading` field is set is a heading row.

## columns
The columns, as records, in their first order: `id` (the field shown, and the column's
name), `title` (the header; the id when absent), `width` (in pixels; 120 when absent),
and optionally `cell` (the name of a class drawn in each of the column's cells), `editor`
(the name of the class a cell becomes while it is edited) or `edit` (`true` for the grid's
own text editor, with `commitOn: "end"` to write once when the edit ends). **The column model follows this list**: add, remove or change a
record and the grid follows.

## headingClass
The name of the class that draws a heading row — `""` for the grid's own, the record's
`heading` text in the theme's muted ink. The class is placed across the row, with the
heading record as its data.

## order
The presented column order, as column ids — `null` means the order of `columns`. Part of
the grid's value-pattern state: delivered through `applyColumnOrder`, so an app that overrides
that owns the order and can persist it.

## widths
Per-column width overrides, `{ id: width }` — `null` means the widths in `columns`. **A
width of `0` drops the column entirely**, which is the deliberate narrow-screen degrade:
priority, not squish. `widths = { app.narrow ? { notes: 0 } : null }` is a responsive
layout.

## sortOn
The column id the sort *names* — `""` when unsorted. **Sorting names a derivation; it
does not reorder anything.** The truth stays in the data: your app derives the sorted items
from this, and the grid displays the order it is given.

## sortDir
`"asc"` or `"desc"`, alongside `sortOn`.

## applySort()
**Delivery seam for sorting.** The default writes `sortOn`/`sortDir`; override it to own
the sort — which is what you do when the sort belongs to a server query.

## applyColumnOrder()
**Delivery seam for reordering**, receiving the new column-id order. Override it to
persist a user's column layout.

## applyColumnWidth()
**Delivery seam for resizing**, receiving a column id and its new width. Widths are
plain values — **nothing measures cells** — so an override is free to clamp, snap, or
store them.

## applyEdit()
An edited cell's value on its way into its record: `applyEdit(record, field, value)` — the
typed text, or a number for a field that holds one (text that is not a number yet is not
sent). The default lets it write. To send the change to a service, override it, start the request, and
return `false` — the record keeps what it had until the service's answer replaces it.

```declare-fragment
DataGrid [ items = { app.rows.value.rows },
    columns = { [ { id: "title", title: "Title", width: 240, edit: true, commitOn: "end" } ] },
    applyEdit(record: object, field: string, value: string) -> boolean {
        app.save(record, field, value)
        return false
        } ]
```

## headerClick()
Toggles the sort on a column: same column flips direction, a new column starts
ascending. Routes through `applySort`.

## headerHeight
The header row's height. The header takes the top of the grid's box and stays there; the
rows begin below it and scroll up under it.

## selectable()
`Table`'s, answered here: a heading record is not selectable, every other record is.
An override narrows it with `super.selectable(m)`, so headings stay out.

## _topInset
**Internal.** Follows `headerHeight`: the header row pinned over the rows' top.

## editing
Whether the active cell is open in its column's editor. Moving the active cell ends it.

## _leaveEdit()
**Internal.** Ends the edit: the keyboard comes back to the grid, on the cell a number of rows down, passing over headings.

## _leaveAcross()
**Internal.** Ends the edit a cell along: a number of columns over, wrapping to the next or previous row past either end.

## _seatKeyboard()
**Internal.** Gives the keyboard to the active cell's text field, its text selected, once the press or move that made it active has finished.

## _within
**Internal.** Whether the keyboard is in the grid — on it, or in a cell's field (`containsFocus`).

## _moveWatch
**Internal.** Ends the edit when the active cell moves.

## _isEditing()
**Internal.** The `when` of the bindings that leave an editor of your own.

## _keyed
**Internal.** Whether the grid is being used from the keyboard: set by a key, cleared by a
press on a row. A grid that is only read and selected outlines its active cell only then.

## _editable
**Internal.** Whether any column is editable (`edit: true`, or an `editor`): the active cell is
then where typing goes, and is outlined whenever the grid has focus.

## _leaveHere()
**Internal.** Escape from an editor of your own: leaves it on the same cell.

## _leaveDown()
**Internal.** Return from an editor of your own: leaves it a row down.

## _leaveNext()
**Internal.** Tab from an editor of your own: leaves it a cell along.

## _leavePrev()
**Internal.** Shift-Tab from an editor of your own: leaves it a cell back.

## keyBindings
Escape and Return leave an editor of your own, and Tab and Shift-Tab leave it a cell along, when it does not answer them itself.

## onFocus
The keyboard arriving on a cell of a text column goes on to its field.

## _columnRecords()
**Internal.** The derived column model: the `columns` records ordered and widthed by state, with running x offsets.

## _currentOrder()
**Internal.** The column ids in their present order.

## _colWidthOf()
**Internal.** The present width of a column by id.

## _dragCol
**Internal.** The column id currently being dragged.

## _dropAt
**Internal.** The index the insertion bar marks, or −1.

## _dropIndexFor()
**Internal.** Which slot a dragged header's centre would land in.

## _dropBarX()
**Internal.** The insertion bar's x for a drop index.

## _commitDrop()
**Internal.** Commits a reorder at release — drop-commit, since swapping under the
pointer re-derives the spans mid-drag and flickers.

## _prevColId()
**Internal.** The column left of a given one, for the resize grip.

## _isDataGrid
**Internal.** The marker rows and headers guard on — not API.

## _firstRow()
**Internal.** The index of the first row that can be stood on, or −1.

## inHeader
Whether the keyboard position is in the header row: Up from the first row goes there,
Left and Right move along the headers, Enter or Space sorts by the column, Down comes back.

## activeCol
The active cell's column — with Table's active row, the grid's keyboard position. Left and Right move it.
The active cell is outlined while the grid has focus: always in a grid with an editable column,
where it is where typing goes; in a grid that is only read and selected, while it is used from
the keyboard, so a press shows the row it selects and no cell.

## _activeCell()
**Internal.** The active cell, when its row is built.

## _enterCell()
**Internal.** Enters a cell: an edit cell's field takes the focus, else its first control does, or for Space is pressed.

## onKeyDown
Left and Right move the active cell; Enter or F2 enters it — into its editor, or a cell
of your own's first control — and Space presses that control; the rest is Table's.
An override passes the keys it does not take to `super.onKeyDown(e)`.

## datapath
The grid's own rows: its `items`, wrapped so each record becomes a row. Set by the grid; not
yours to point elsewhere.
*/

include [ "table.declare", "textinput.declare" ]

class DataGrid extends Table [
    accessibilityRole = grid,

    // the marker rows and headers guard on

    _isDataGrid: boolean = true,

    headerHeight: number = { provided("theme").gridHeaderHeight },

    // ── what it shows: the records, and the columns as records ─────────────

    items: array = null,
    columns: array = [],
    headingClass: string = "",

    // ── the column-model state — the value pattern ────────────────────────

    order: array = null,        // column ids, presented order (null = as written)
    widths: object = null,      // { id: width } overrides (null = as written)
    sortOn: string = "",        // the column id the sort names ("" = unsorted)
    sortDir: string = "",       // "asc" | "desc"

    // ── the delivery seams — defaults write the attributes; use-site overrides
    //    own the state (persisted layouts, app-derived sorts) ──────────────

    applySort(column: string, direction: string) { sortOn = column; sortDir = direction },
    applyColumnOrder(ord: array) { order = ord },
    applyColumnWidth(id: string, w: number) { widths = ({ ...(widths ?? ({})), [id]: w }) },

    // an edited cell's text, on its way into the record: the default lets it
    // write; an override sends it (a PATCH) and returns false to leave the
    // record as it was until the service's answer replaces it

    applyEdit(record: object, field: string, value: string | number) -> boolean { return true },

    headerClick(id: string) {
        this.applySort(id, sortOn == id && sortDir == "asc" ? "desc" : "asc")
        },

    // a heading between rows is not a row a person picks

    selectable(m: object) -> boolean { return m == null || (m as any).heading == null },

    // ── the column records: `columns`, ordered and widthed by the state, with
    //    running x offsets — the one derived truth every header cell and grid
    //    cell positions from. The inputs ride in as arguments, so the deriving
    //    constraint tracks them ───────────────────────────────────────────

    _columnRecords(cols: array, ord: array, w: object) -> array {
        const list = []
        for (const c of (cols ?? [])) { if (c != null && c.id != null) list.push(c) }
        if (ord != null) {
            const rank = (c) => {
                const i = ord.indexOf("" + c.id)
                return i < 0 ? ord.length + list.indexOf(c) : i
                }
            list.sort((a, b) => rank(a) - rank(b))
            }
        const out = []
        let x = 0
        for (const c of list) {
            const id = "" + c.id
            const wd = w != null && typeof (w as any)[id] == "number" ? (w as any)[id] : (typeof c.width == "number" ? c.width : 120)
            // width 0 drops the column — the deliberate narrow degrade
            // (priority, not squish): a responsive `widths` state zeroes the
            // low-priority columns and the model omits them entirely
            if (wd <= 0) continue
            // `editor` names what a cell becomes while it is edited; `edit: true` is the grid's own text editor
            const editor = c.editor != null ? "" + c.editor : c.edit == true ? "DataGridEdit" : ""
            out.push({ id: id, title: "" + (c.title ?? id), width: wd, x: x,
                cell: c.cell != null ? "" + c.cell : "", editor: editor, commitOn: c.commitOn == "end" ? "end" : "", i: out.length })
            x = x + wd
            }
        return out
        },

    _currentOrder() -> array { return this._colData.value.cols.map((r) => r.id) },

    // the column model as a dataset, which the header and every row read

    _colData: Dataset [ contents = { { cols: classroot._columnRecords(classroot.columns, classroot.order, classroot.widths) } } ],

    // the rows: one per item, replicated over the items as a dataset that holds
    // them (so a row's write lands in their source); a heading record is a
    // heading row

    // the items, as the dataset the rows replicate over; it holds the records it is
    // given, so a write through a row lands in their source

    _rowsData: Dataset [ contents = { { rows: classroot.items ?? [] } } ],
    datapath = { this._rowsData.value },

    // the header row's place at the top of the grid: the rows stack after it,
    // and scroll up under the header itself (`_hdr`, pinned)

    _hdrSpace: View [ width = 0, height = { classroot.headerHeight } ],
    _topInset = { this.headerHeight },

    TableRow [ datapath = :rows[], virtualize = true,
        classFor = { :heading != null ? DataGridHeadingRow : DataGridRow } ],

    // reorder-by-drag commits on release, never live: swapping under the
    // pointer would re-derive the spans mid-drag and flicker. While a header
    // drags, `_dragCol`/`_dropAt` drive the insertion bar (the vertical divider
    // at the would-be slot), and the order commits at the drop.

    _dragCol: string = "",
    _dropAt: number = -1,

    _dropIndexFor(cx: number) -> number {
        const cols = this._colData.value.cols
        for (let i = 0; i < cols.length; i = i + 1) {
            if (cx < cols[i].x + cols[i].width / 2) return i
            }
        return cols.length
        },

    // `at` rides in as an argument so the insertion bar's constraint tracks
    // it, as `_columnRecords` takes its state

    _dropBarX(at: number) -> number {
        const cols = this._colData.value.cols
        if (at < 0) return -10
        if (at >= cols.length) {
            const last = cols[cols.length - 1]
            return last != null ? last.x + last.width : 0
            }
        return cols[at].x
        },

    _commitDrop(id: string) {
        const cols = this._colData.value.cols
        const from = cols.findIndex((r) => r.id == id)
        const at = _dropAt
        _dragCol = ""
        _dropAt = -1
        if (from < 0 || at < 0) return
        const to = at > from ? at - 1 : at
        if (to == from) return
        const ord = cols.map((r) => r.id)
        ord.splice(from, 1)
        ord.splice(to, 0, id)
        this.applyColumnOrder(ord)
        },

    // ── the active cell: the keyboard position in two dimensions — the active
    //    row is Table's, the column is this. Left/Right move the column; Enter,
    //    F2 or a typed character enters the cell; inside it, Tab walks the row's
    //    cells and Esc comes back (planes.md §13.6.2) ──────────────────────

    activeCol: number = 0,

    // the keyboard position may be in the header row: Up from the first row goes
    // there, Left and Right move along the headers, Enter or Space sorts by the
    // column, Down comes back to the rows

    inHeader: boolean = false,

    // whether the active cell is worth outlining: in a grid a person edits, it is
    // where typing goes, so always; in one they only read and select, it is the
    // keyboard's place, so while they use the keys (a press on a row clears it)

    _keyed: boolean = false,
    _editable: boolean = { this._colData.value.cols.some((c) => (c.editor ?? "") != "" || c.edit === true) },

    _firstRow() -> number {
        const n = this.itemCount()
        for (let i = 0; i < n; i = i + 1) { if (this.selectable(this.itemAt(i))) return i }
        return -1
        },

    _activeCell() -> DataGridCell? {
        const inst = this.children.find((c) => (c as DataGridRow)._isGridRow === true && (c as DataGridRow).tableIndex() == activeIndex)
        if (inst == null) return null
        return (inst as DataGridRow).cellAt(activeCol)
        },

    // ── editing: the active cell open in its column's editor. A press selects a
    //    cell; Enter, F2 or a second press enters it; Escape or Enter leaves.
    //    Moving the active cell ends the edit ──────────────────────────────

    editing: boolean = false,

    // whether the keyboard is in the grid — on it, or in a cell's field

    _within: boolean = { this.containsFocus },

    // the keyboard coming to the grid, on a cell its text editor edits: the
    // cell's field takes it, its text selected, so typing reaches the cell

    onFocus() {
        super.onFocus()
        this._seatKeyboard()
        },

    // the active cell's text field takes the keyboard from the grid, its text
    // selected so what is typed replaces it — after the press or the move that
    // made it active has finished, so the platform's own focus does not follow

    _seatKeyboard() {
        afterDelay(0, () => {
            if (!this.focused || editing || inHeader) return
            const ed = this._activeCell()?._editor()
            if (ed == null) return
            Focus.focus(ed)
            afterSettle(() => { ed.select(0, ("" + ed.text).length) })
            })
        },
    _moveWatch: Node [
        at: string = { classroot.activeIndex + ":" + classroot.activeCol },
        trackChanges = ["at"],
        onChange() { classroot.editing = false }
        ],

    _enterCell(cell: DataGridCell, typed: string) {
        if (cell._kind() == "editor") {
            editing = true
            // the editor is built by this settle: its first control takes the focus,
            // and a text field there starts with its value selected, so what is
            // typed replaces it (a combobox's entry as much as a plain field)
            afterSettle(() => {
                const ed = cell._editor()
                if (ed != null) { Focus.focus(ed); ed.select(0, ("" + ed.text).length); return }
                const ctl = cell._firstControl()
                if (ctl == null) return
                Focus.focus(ctl)
                afterSettle(() => {
                    const f = Focus.getFocus()
                    if (f instanceof TextInput) f.select(0, ("" + f.text).length)
                    })
                })
            return
            }
        const ctl = cell._firstControl()
        if (ctl == null) return
        if (typed == " ") (ctl as Control).press()
        else Focus.focus(ctl)
        },

    // the edit ends: the keyboard comes back to the grid, on the cell `d` rows
    // down (0: the same one), passing over headings

    _leaveEdit(d: number) {
        editing = false
        Focus.focus(this)
        if (d == 0) return
        const n = this.itemCount()
        let i = activeIndex + d
        while (i >= 0 && i < n && !this.selectable(this.itemAt(i))) i = i + d
        if (i < 0 || i >= n) return
        activeIndex = i
        activeItem = this.itemAt(i)
        this.showRow(i)
        },

    // the edit ends a cell along: `d` columns over (1 right, -1 left), on to
    // the next row's first cell past the last column and back the same way

    _leaveAcross(d: number) {
        const n = this._colData.value.cols.length
        const c = activeCol + d
        if (c >= 0 && c < n) { this._leaveEdit(0); activeCol = c; return }
        const before = activeIndex
        this._leaveEdit(d)
        if (activeIndex != before) activeCol = d > 0 ? 0 : n - 1
        },

    // what an editor of your own does not answer on its way out through the
    // walk: Escape leaves it, Return leaves it a row down, Tab and Shift-Tab a
    // cell along

    keyBindings = { [ ({ key: "Escape", command: "_leaveHere", when: "_isEditing" }),
        ({ key: "Enter", command: "_leaveDown", when: "_isEditing" }),
        ({ key: "Tab", command: "_leaveNext", when: "_isEditing" }),
        ({ key: "Shift+Tab", command: "_leavePrev", when: "_isEditing" }) ] },
    _isEditing() -> boolean { return editing },
    _leaveHere() { this._leaveEdit(0) },
    _leaveDown() { this._leaveEdit(1) },
    _leaveNext() { this._leaveAcross(1) },
    _leavePrev() { this._leaveAcross(-1) },

    onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
        // the grid's own keys; the rest go on to Table's, and on through the walk
        _keyed = true
        if (disabled) { super.onKeyDown(e); return }
        const n = this._colData.value.cols.length
        const plain = !e.meta && !e.ctrl && !e.alt
        if (e.key == "ArrowRight" && plain) { activeCol = Math.min(n - 1, activeCol + 1); return }
        if (e.key == "ArrowLeft" && plain) { activeCol = Math.max(0, activeCol - 1); return }
        if (inHeader) {
            if (e.key == "ArrowDown" && plain) { inHeader = false; return }
            if ((e.key == "Enter" || e.key == " ") && plain) {
                const c = this._colData.value.cols[activeCol]
                if (c != null && !e.repeat) this.headerClick("" + c.id)
                return
                }
            // the keys the rows would take stop at the header; the rest go on
            if (e.key == "ArrowUp" || e.key == "Home" || e.key == "End" || e.key == "Delete" || e.key == "Backspace") return
            }
        if (e.key == "ArrowUp" && plain && activeIndex <= this._firstRow()) { inHeader = true; return }
        const cell = this._activeCell()
        // a key an input method is composing is the method's, never a command
        if (cell != null && !e.repeat && !e.isComposing) {
            const kind = cell._kind()
            if ((e.key == "Enter" || e.key == "F2") && kind != "text" && plain) { this._enterCell(cell, ""); return }
            if (e.key == " " && kind == "own" && plain) { this._enterCell(cell, " "); return }
            }
        super.onKeyDown(e)
        },

    _prevColId(id: string) -> string {
        const cols = this._colData.value.cols
        const i = cols.findIndex((r) => r.id == id)
        return i > 0 ? cols[i - 1].id : ""
        },

    _colWidthOf(id: string) -> number {
        const cols = this._colData.value.cols
        const r = cols.find((c) => c.id == id)
        return r != null ? r.width : 0
        },

    // ── the header: the top of the grid's own box, pinned while the rows
    //    scroll (ignoreScroll), drawn over them (it comes after the rows). The
    //    rows stack after `_hdrSpace`, its place; ignoreLayout keeps the header
    //    itself out of the stacking. A grid placed by a layout is header and
    //    rows together, and covers nothing beside it ──────────────────────────

    _hdr: View [ height = { classroot.headerHeight },
        x = 0, y = 0,
        width = { classroot.width },
        ignoreLayout = true,
        ignoreScroll = true,
        fill = { provided("theme").surface },
        datapath = { classroot._colData.value },
        accessibilityRole = row,

        View [ datapath = :cols[], claim = x,
            colid: string = { "" + :id },

            // a column header: named by its title, saying how its column is sorted;
            // the keyboard's position when the grid's is in the header row

            accessibilityRole = columnHeader,
            accessibilitySort = { (classroot as DataGrid).sortOn != colid ? "none" : (classroot as DataGrid).sortDir == "desc" ? "descending" : "ascending" },
            _ci: number = { 0 + (:i ?? 0) },
            activeHere: boolean = { (classroot as DataGrid).focused && (classroot as DataGrid).inHeader && (classroot as DataGrid).activeCol == _ci },
            trackChanges = ["activeHere"],
            onChange(e: ChangeEvent) {
                const g = classroot as DataGrid
                if (activeHere) g._activeView = this
                else if (g._activeView == this) g._activeView = null
                },
            x = { dragging && moved ? dragX : :x },
            width = { :width },
            height = 100%,

            // dragging lifts the header: an opaque ghost above its
            // neighbors (raise + surface fill + edge), tracking the pointer;
            // the grid's insertion bar shows the would-be slot; the order
            // commits at release (drop-commit — no mid-drag re-derives)

            dragging: boolean = false,
            moved: boolean = false,
            dragX: number = 0,
            x0: number = 0,

            // the lifted ghost is unmistakably in hand: an accent-edged,
            // shadowed, rounded card floating over its neighbors

            fill = { dragging && moved ? provided("theme").surface : null },
            stroke = { dragging && moved ? Stroke(1.5, provided("theme").accent) : activeHere ? Stroke(2, provided("theme").accent) : null },
            shadow = { dragging && moved ? (provided("theme").dragShadow) : null },
            cornerRadius = { dragging && moved ? 6 : activeHere ? provided("theme").gridCellCornerRadius : 0 },
            opacity = { dragging && moved ? 0.95 : 1 },

            // a press that stays put is the click — the sort; one that wanders
            // drags the column (the runtime tells them apart)

            onClick() { if ((classroot as DataGrid)._isDataGrid === true) (classroot as DataGrid).headerClick(colid) },
            onDragStart(e: PointerEvent) {
                if ((classroot as DataGrid)._isDataGrid !== true) return
                dragging = true
                moved = true
                x0 = this.x
                dragX = this.x + e.deltaX
                this.raise()
                },
            onDrag(e: PointerEvent) {
                if (!dragging) return
                dragX = x0 + e.deltaX
                ;(classroot as DataGrid)._dragCol = colid
                ;(classroot as DataGrid)._dropAt = (classroot as DataGrid)._dropIndexFor(dragX + :width / 2)
                },
            onDragEnd(e: DragEndEvent) {
                if (!dragging) return
                dragging = false
                moved = false
                if (!e.canceled) (classroot as DataGrid)._commitDrop(colid)
                else { (classroot as DataGrid)._dragCol = ""; (classroot as DataGrid)._dropAt = -1 }
                },

            lbl: TextLabel [ x = 8, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridHeaderFontSize }, fontWeight = semibold, wrap = false,
                textColor = { provided("theme").textMuted },
                text = { "" + :title } ],

            // The sort direction, drawn: one chevron class, turned by its
            // `dir`, so a sort header agrees with every other disclosure mark
            // in the chrome.

            sortMark: ChevronIcon [ y = center, iconSize = 11,
                x = { parent.width - this.width - 14 },
                visible = { (classroot as DataGrid).sortOn == parent.colid },
                ink = { provided("theme").textMuted },
                dir = { (classroot as DataGrid).sortDir == "desc" ? "down" : "up" } ],

            // the resize grip: the left-edge sliver, resizing the previous
            // column — the boundary region belongs to this cell (later
            // siblings hit first), so the grip must live here to be
            // reachable; absent on the first column. Plain widths delivered
            // through applyColumnWidth — nothing measures anything.

            grip: View [ width = 10, height = 100%,
                x = -5,
                visible = { (:x ?? 0) > 0 },
                cursor = "col-resize", claim = x,
                resizing: boolean = false,
                pid: string = "",
                w0: number = 0,
                onDragStart(e: PointerEvent) {
                    if ((classroot as DataGrid)._isDataGrid !== true) return
                    pid = (classroot as DataGrid)._prevColId(this.parent.colid)
                    if (pid == "") return
                    w0 = (classroot as DataGrid)._colWidthOf(pid)
                    resizing = true
                    },
                onDrag(e: PointerEvent) {
                    if (!resizing) return
                    (classroot as DataGrid).applyColumnWidth(pid, Math.max(40, w0 + e.deltaX))
                    },
                onDragEnd(e: DragEndEvent) {
                    if (resizing && e.canceled) (classroot as DataGrid).applyColumnWidth(pid, w0)
                    resizing = false
                    }
                ],

            hair: View [ width = 1, height = { parent.height - 10 }, y = 5,
                x = { parent.width - 1 }, fill = { provided("theme").line } ]
            ],

        // the insertion bar — where the dragged column would land

        drop: View [ width = 2, y = 2, height = { parent.height - 4 },
            visible = { (classroot as DataGrid)._dragCol != "" },
            x = { (classroot as DataGrid)._dropBarX((classroot as DataGrid)._dropAt) - 1 },
            fill = { provided("theme").accent } ],

        // the hairline under the header. It stays a child view rather than
        // the header's own bottom stroke: a per-side stroke is a bare list,
        // and its colour here is a theme read.

        rule: View [ x = 0, width = 100%, height = 1,
            y = { parent.height - 1 }, fill = { provided("theme").line } ]
        ]
    ]


// ── The rows ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/*
# DataGridRow

**Internal.** A row of a `DataGrid`, one per item, holding one `DataGridCell` per column
placed from the column model.

## rec
The row's record, as a derived attribute, so its cells follow the record's changes.

## _grid
**Internal.** The `DataGrid` it belongs to.

## cellAt()
The cell in a column, by its place in the column model.

## _reportsActive
**Internal.** Off: in a grid the keyboard is on a cell, and the cell reports itself, not the row.

## _isGridRow
**Internal.** The marker identifying a grid row — not API.

## _colAt()
**Internal.** The column at an x in the row, or −1.

## _pressedActive
**Internal.** Whether the press began on the grid's active cell — what makes it a second press.

## onPointerDown
Notes whether the press is on the active cell, then focuses the grid as any table row does.

## onClick
Selects the cell under the press; a second press on the selected cell enters it.

## height
From the theme's `gridRowHeight` (28 by default); the header row is `gridHeaderHeight` (30).
*/

class DataGridRow extends TableRow [
    accessibilityRole = row,
    _isGridRow: boolean = true,
    _reportsActive = false,          // in a grid the keyboard is on a cell, which reports itself

    height = { provided("theme").gridRowHeight },

    rec: object = { this.datapath != null ? :@ : null },
    _grid: DataGrid? = { (this.parent as DataGrid)._isDataGrid === true ? this.parent as DataGrid : null },

    // one cell per column: its place and what it draws, and the row's record
    // in a list of its own — the record itself, never a copy, so what a cell
    // writes lands in the record

    // the row's cells as a dataset: for each column, its place and what it draws, with
    // the row's record — the record itself, so a cell's write lands in it. A row
    // presenting no record (parked) draws nothing: a cell class is never built
    // over a null record

    _cellData: Dataset [ contents = { { cells: classroot._grid == null ? [] : classroot._grid._colData.value.cols.map((c) =>
        ({ id: c.id, title: c.title, x: c.x, width: c.width, cell: c.cell, editor: c.editor, commitOn: c.commitOn, i: c.i,
            row: classroot.rec == null ? [] : [classroot.rec] })) } } ],

    cellAt(col: number) -> DataGridCell? {
        return (this._cells.children.find((c) => (c as DataGridCell)._isGridCell === true && (c as DataGridCell)._ci == col) ?? null) as DataGridCell | null
        },

    // the column at x in the row, or -1

    _colAt(x: number) -> number {
        const g = this._grid
        if (g == null) return -1
        const c = g._colData.value.cols.find((r) => x >= r.x && x < r.x + r.width)
        return c != null ? c.i : -1
        },

    // a press selects the cell under it; a second press on the active cell
    // enters it. Whether it was active is read at the press, before the
    // press itself moves anything

    _pressedActive: boolean = false,
    onPointerDown(e?: PointerEvent) {
        const g = this._grid
        const ci = e != null ? this._colAt(e.x) : -1
        _pressedActive = g != null && g._within && !g.inHeader && this.active && ci >= 0 && ci == g.activeCol
        if (g != null) g._keyed = false
        super.onPointerDown()
        },
    onClick(e?: PointerEvent) {
        const again = _pressedActive
        _pressedActive = false
        super.onClick(e)
        const g = this._grid
        const ci = e != null ? this._colAt(e.x) : -1
        if (g == null || ci < 0 || disabled) return
        g.activeCol = ci
        g.inHeader = false
        if (again && !g.editing) {
            const cell = this.cellAt(ci)
            if (cell != null && cell._kind() == "editor") g._enterCell(cell, "")
            }
        else g._seatKeyboard()
        },

    _cells: View [ x = 0, y = 0, width = 100%, height = 100%,
        datapath = { classroot._cellData.value },
        DataGridCell [ datapath = :cells[] ]
        ]
    ]


/*
# DataGridHeadingRow

**Internal.** The row a `DataGrid` makes for a record with a `heading` field, drawing the
grid's `headingClass` or `DataGridHeading` across the full width.

## rec
The heading record.

## _grid
**Internal.** The `DataGrid` it belongs to.

## _isGridHeading
**Internal.** The marker identifying a heading row — not API.

## height
The theme's `gridRowHeight`, the same as an ordinary row.

## _kind
**Internal.** The class the heading row draws: the grid's `headingClass`, else
`DataGridHeading`.
*/

class DataGridHeadingRow extends TableRow [
    accessibilityRole = row,
    _isGridHeading: boolean = true,
    _reportsActive = false,
    focusable = false,
    height = { provided("theme").gridRowHeight },
    rec: object = { this.datapath != null ? :@ : null },
    _grid: DataGrid? = { (this.parent as DataGrid)._isDataGrid === true ? this.parent as DataGrid : null },
    _kind: string = { this._grid != null && this._grid.headingClass != "" ? this._grid.headingClass : "DataGridHeading" },

    // the heading record in a list of its own, for the class that draws it

    _headData: Dataset [ contents = { { one: [classroot.rec] } } ],
    View [ width = 100%, height = 100%, datapath = { classroot._headData.value },
        kind: string = { classroot._kind },
        View [ datapath = :one[], width = 100%, height = 100%,
            classFor = { parent.kind == "DataGridHeading" ? DataGridHeading : parent.kind } ]
        ]
    ]


/*
# DataGridHeading

**Internal.** The grid's own heading: the record's `heading` text, semibold, in the
theme's muted ink.
*/

class DataGridHeading [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
    TextLabel [ x = 8, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize }, fontWeight = semibold, wrap = false,
        textColor = { provided("theme").textMuted }, text = { "" + (:heading ?? "") } ]
    ]


// ── The cells ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/*
# DataGridCell

One cell of a `DataGrid` row: placed and sized from its column, with the row's record as the
data of what it draws — the column's `cell` class, an edit field for an `edit` column, or the
field as text. The grid makes it; a program writes the class it draws.

## _kind()
**Internal.** What the cell holds: `"editor"` (its column edits in an editor), `"own"` (the column's class) or `"text"`.

## _editorCls
**Internal.** The class its column edits in, or `""`.

## _shows
**Internal.** The class it shows when not edited: its column's `cell`, or the field as text.

## _atCell
**Internal.** Whether it is the grid's active cell, focused or not.

## editingHere
Whether it is the cell being edited.

## _drawn()
**Internal.** What the cell draws, as a view: its one child view.

## _editor()
**Internal.** The grid's text field in the cell, when it holds one, or null.

## _firstControl()
**Internal.** The first focusable view inside the cell's own class that a person can see (shown, and not fully transparent), or null.

## _writeText()
**Internal.** Writes a text value into the cell's field in its row's record.

## _dataGrid
**Internal.** The `DataGrid` its row belongs to.

## activeHere
Whether it is the grid's active cell while the grid has focus.

## _outlined
**Internal.** Whether the active cell is outlined: always in a grid with an editable column,
and in one only read and selected, while it is used from the keyboard.

## _takeActive()
**Internal.** Makes this the grid's active cell, as its edit field does when it takes the focus.

## _isGridCell
**Internal.** The marker identifying a grid cell — not API.

## _ci
**Internal.** Its column's place in the column model — what the grid's `activeCol` names.

## _commitOn
**Internal.** The column's `commitOn`.

## column
The column this cell is in, as `{ id, title }` — what a cell class shared by several
columns reads, as `provided("column")`.

## _fld
**Internal.** The field its column shows.

## _cls
**Internal.** The class it draws: its editor while edited (and the grid's text editor while it is the active cell), else what it shows.

## _reportActive()
**Internal.** The cell that becomes the grid's active cell records itself as the grid's
`_activeView`, which the grid names to a screen reader; one that stops being it takes itself
back.

## x
Its column's offset in the row.

## width
Its column's width.

## stroke
The outline that marks the active cell.

## cornerRadius
From the theme's `gridCellCornerRadius` (3 by default): the active cell's outline.

## _row
**Internal.** The `DataGridRow` this cell is drawn in, or null outside one.

## gridFocused
Whether the grid holds keyboard focus in its body (not its header), so a cell can show
where the keyboard is.
*/

class DataGridCell [ x = { :x }, width = { :width }, height = 100%, clip = true,
    accessibilityRole = cell,
    _isGridCell: boolean = true,
    _ci: number = { 0 + (:i ?? 0) },
    _fld: string = { "" + :id },
    _commitOn: string = { "" + (:commitOn ?? "") },

    // which column this cell is in, for a cell class shared by several:
    // `provided("column")` from inside it

    column: object = { { id: "" + :id, title: "" + (:title ?? :id) } },
    _editorCls: string = { "" + (:editor ?? "") },
    _shows: string = { ("" + (:cell ?? "")) != "" ? "" + :cell : "DataGridText" },

    // what it draws: its column's editor while it is edited; the grid's text
    // editor while it is the active cell, too, so typed text — an accent, an
    // input method's word — arrives in a field and opens the edit; else what
    // the column shows

    _cls: string = { this.editingHere || (this._editorCls == "DataGridEdit" && this.activeHere) ? this._editorCls : this._shows },

    // "editor": its column edits in an editor; "own": it draws a class of the
    // program's, entered at its first control; "text": neither

    _kind() -> string { return this._editorCls != "" ? "editor" : this._shows == "DataGridText" ? "text" : "own" },

    // what it draws, as a view: its one child view

    _drawn() -> View? { return this.childViews.length > 0 ? this.childViews[0] : null },

    _editor() -> TextInput? {
        const d = this._drawn()
        return d != null && (d as DataGridEdit)._isGridEdit === true ? (d as DataGridEdit)._ed : null
        },

    _firstControl() -> View? {
        const d = this._drawn()
        if (d == null) return null
        const walk = (v: View) => {
            for (const c of v.childViews) {
                // what a person can't see is not where the keyboard goes
                if (c.focusable == true && c.visible && c.opacity > 0) return c
                const deeper = walk(c)
                if (deeper != null) return deeper
                }
            return null
            }
        return walk(d)
        },

    _writeText(v: string) {
        const d = this._drawn()
        if (d != null && (d as DataGridEdit)._isGridEdit === true) (d as DataGridEdit).write(v)
        },

    // the active cell — the grid's keyboard position — is outlined while the
    // grid has focus

    _row: DataGridRow? = { (this.parent?.parent as DataGridRow)?._isGridRow === true ? this.parent.parent as DataGridRow : null },
    _dataGrid: DataGrid? = { this._row != null ? this._row._grid : null },
    gridFocused: boolean = { this._dataGrid != null && this._dataGrid._within && !this._dataGrid.inHeader },
    _atCell: boolean = { this._row?.active == true && this._dataGrid?.activeCol == this._ci },
    activeHere: boolean = { this.gridFocused && this._atCell },

    // the outline: always where typing goes (a grid with an editable column); in a
    // grid only read and selected, while it is used from the keyboard, so a press
    // shows the row it selects and no cell

    _outlined: boolean = { this.activeHere
        && (this._dataGrid?._editable == true || this._dataGrid?._keyed == true || this._dataGrid?.focusVisible == true) },
    editingHere: boolean = { this._atCell && this._dataGrid?.editing == true },
    stroke = { this._outlined ? Stroke(2, provided("theme").accent) : null },
    cornerRadius = { provided("theme").gridCellCornerRadius },

    // the active cell says so to its grid, which names it to a screen reader —
    // heard by this member, so a cell class's own `trackChanges` and `onChange`
    // are its own

    _watch: Node [
        on: boolean = { classroot.activeHere },
        trackChanges = ["on"],
        onChange() { classroot._reportActive() }
        ],
    _reportActive() {
        const g = this._dataGrid
        if (g == null) return
        if (this.activeHere) g._activeView = this
        else if (g._activeView == this) g._activeView = null
        },

    // a control in the cell taking focus — by a press or by Tab inside the row —
    // makes this the active cell

    _takeActive() {
        const g = this._dataGrid
        const r = this._row
        if (g == null || r == null) return
        g.activeCol = this._ci
        const i = r.tableIndex()
        g.activeIndex = i
        g.activeItem = g.itemAt(i)
        },

    // what it draws: one view over the row's record, of the class its column
    // names

    View [ datapath = :row[], width = 100%, height = 100%,
        classFor = { parent._cls == "DataGridEdit" ? DataGridEdit : parent._cls == "DataGridText" ? DataGridText : parent._cls } ]
    ]


/*
# DataGridText

**Internal.** What a `DataGrid` column with no `cell` draws: its field, as text.

## field
The field shown — its column's id.
*/

class DataGridText [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
    field: string = { (parent as DataGridCell)._isGridCell === true ? (parent as DataGridCell)._fld : "" },
    _t: TextLabel [ x = 8, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize }, wrap = false,
        text = { "" + (:@[(classroot.field)] ?? "") } ]
    ]


/*
# DataGridEdit

**Internal.** What an `edit: true` column of a `DataGrid` draws: a text field writing its
field into the row's record as you type.

## field
The field edited — its column's id.

## write()
Writes a text value into the field — through the grid's `applyEdit`, which may keep it.

## open
Whether the edit is open: the field shows as a field. Until then the active cell's field holds the keyboard with its text selected, drawn as the cell's text.

## _cell
**Internal.** The cell it is drawn in.

## _typing
**Internal.** Text arriving in the field opens the edit.

## onInit
Gives the keyboard to the field when the cell is the active cell of a grid that has it.

## _grid
**Internal.** The grid the cell is in.

## _before
**Internal.** The text when the edit began, which Esc puts back.

## _isGridEdit
**Internal.** The marker identifying an edit cell's field — not API.
*/

class DataGridEdit [ width = 100%, height = 100%,
    _isGridEdit: boolean = true,
    _cell: DataGridCell? = { (parent as DataGridCell)._isGridCell === true ? parent as DataGridCell : null },
    field: string = { this._cell != null ? this._cell._fld : "" },
    _before: string = "",
    _grid: DataGrid? = { this._cell != null ? this._cell._dataGrid : null },

    // the edit is open: the field shows as a field. Until then the active cell's
    // field holds the keyboard with its text selected, drawn as the cell's text,
    // so what is typed replaces it and opens the edit

    open: boolean = { this._cell != null && (this._cell.editingHere || (this._ed.editing && this._cell._atCell)) },

    // the field keeps the value's kind: a number field takes the typed text as a
    // number, and text that is not one yet (a "-", an empty field) writes nothing

    write(v: string) {
        const rec = :@
        const now = (rec as any)[field]
        let out: string | number = v
        if (typeof now == "number") {
            const n = v.trim() == "" ? NaN : Number(v)
            if (!Number.isFinite(n)) return
            out = n
            }
        if (_grid == null || _grid.applyEdit(rec, field, out) !== false) { :@[(field)] = out }
        },

    // built for the active cell of a grid that has the keyboard: the field takes it

    onInit() { if (_grid != null) _grid._seatKeyboard() },

    // text arriving opens the edit

    _typing: Node [
        on: boolean = { classroot._ed.editing },
        trackChanges = ["on"],
        onChange() { if (on && classroot._grid != null && !classroot._grid.editing) classroot._grid.editing = true }
        ],

    _ed: TextInput [ x = 4, y = 3, fontSize = { provided("theme").gridFontSize },
        accessibilityLabel = { classroot._cell != null ? "" + classroot._cell.column.title : "" },
        width = { parent.width - 8 },
        height = { parent.height - 6 },
        padding = [0, 4, 0, 4],
        fill = { classroot.open ? provided("theme").surface : null },
        showsCaret = { classroot.open },
        stroke = { classroot.open ? Stroke(1, provided("theme").accent) : null },
        value = { "" + (:@[(classroot.field)] ?? "") },
        commitOn = { classroot._cell != null && classroot._cell._commitOn == "end" ? "end" : "each" },
        onFocus() {
            super.onFocus()
            classroot._before = "" + (:@[(classroot.field)] ?? "")
            if (classroot._cell != null) classroot._cell._takeActive()
            },
        commit(v: string) { classroot.write(v) },

        // ⌘A on a selected cell is the grid's, every row; in an open edit it is
        // the field's, its text. A field's own binding comes before its editing
        // chords, which ⌘A is one of

        keyBindings = { [ ({ key: "Ctrl+A", command: "_selectRows", when: "_rowsChord" }) ] },
        _rowsChord() -> boolean { return !classroot.open && classroot._grid != null && classroot._grid.selects == "multi" },
        _selectRows() { if (classroot._grid != null) classroot._grid.selectAll() },

        // a second press on the active cell opens the edit, the caret where it landed

        onPointerDown(e?: PointerEvent) {
            if (!classroot.open && classroot._grid != null) classroot._grid.editing = true
            },
        onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
            const g = classroot._grid
            // a key that belongs to an input method's composition (its Return commits it) is the field's
            if (g == null || e.isComposing) { super.onKeyDown(e); return }
            const plain = !e.meta && !e.ctrl && !e.alt
            if (!classroot.open) {
                // selected, not editing: the grid's keys move and enter; the rest is typing
                if ((e.key == "Enter" || e.key == "F2") && plain) { g.editing = true; this.select(0, ("" + this.text).length); return }
                if (e.key == "ArrowUp" || e.key == "ArrowDown" || e.key == "ArrowLeft" || e.key == "ArrowRight" ||
                    e.key == "Home" || e.key == "End" || e.key == "PageUp" || e.key == "PageDown") {
                    Focus.focus(g)
                    g.onKeyDown(e)
                    return
                    }
                super.onKeyDown(e)
                return
                }
            // open: Return and the vertical arrows end the edit and move a row
            if (e.key == "Enter" && plain) { this.endEdit(); g._leaveEdit(1); return }
            if (e.key == "ArrowDown" && plain) { this.endEdit(); g._leaveEdit(1); return }
            if (e.key == "ArrowUp" && plain) { this.endEdit(); g._leaveEdit(-1); return }
            // Tab ends the edit and opens the next cell's place; Shift-Tab the one before
            if (e.key == "Tab" && plain) { this.endEdit(); g._leaveAcross(e.shift ? -1 : 1); return }
            // Escape: a cell that commits at the end drops the edit; one that
            // wrote as it went puts the text back. Either way the edit ends
            if (e.key == "Escape") {
                if (commitOn != "end") classroot.write(classroot._before)
                this.cancelEdit()
                g._leaveEdit(0)
                return
                }
            super.onKeyDown(e)
            }
        ]
    ]
